1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,400
The house that Ruth built.

2
00:00:04,280 --> 00:00:05,840
The eighth wonder of the world.

3
00:00:06,780 --> 00:00:08,200
Baseball stadiums.

4
00:00:08,700 --> 00:00:10,040
American icons.

5
00:00:10,880 --> 00:00:11,580
Sandlocks.

6
00:00:13,340 --> 00:00:14,520
Wooden ballparks.

7
00:00:15,840 --> 00:00:17,600
Multipurpose stadiums.

8
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:19,300
Concrete.

9
00:00:19,720 --> 00:00:20,420
Steel.

10
00:00:21,460 --> 00:00:23,640
Now, baseball parks.

11
00:00:23,641 --> 00:00:26,040
On Modern Marvels.

12
00:00:50,980 --> 00:00:56,140
Baseball, the national pastime, making the good times memorable and the bad times bearable.

13
00:00:56,141 --> 00:01:00,480
the childhood heroes, the smell of the grass,

14
00:01:01,020 --> 00:01:02,880
the crack of the bat,

15
00:01:03,400 --> 00:01:07,580
and the memories of warm summer days spent at the ballpark.

16
00:01:08,920 --> 00:01:11,180
It really is something that just overwhelms you,

17
00:01:11,280 --> 00:01:13,760
it fills you with some sort of emotion

18
00:01:13,761 --> 00:01:15,860
when you step out from underneath the stands

19
00:01:15,861 --> 00:01:17,500
and that field is before you.

20
00:01:17,501 --> 00:01:21,600
And certainly the imagery that the ballpark embeds on you,

21
00:01:21,840 --> 00:01:23,880
in your mind's eye, stays with you forever.

22
00:01:23,881 --> 00:01:30,880
The parks are part of the enduring legacy of baseball, each adding its own imprint to our memories.

23
00:01:30,881 --> 00:01:36,880
These ballparks, they're almost monuments to the game, even though they're living things.

24
00:01:36,881 --> 00:01:42,880
They were truly monuments to the glory of America's national pastime.

25
00:01:53,880 --> 00:02:01,520
Today's games are held in technological wonders.

26
00:02:02,280 --> 00:02:08,480
The fans experiencing the game firsthand will be subjected to blinding lights, ear-splitting

27
00:02:08,481 --> 00:02:15,220
noises and fiery explosions, all of which heighten the baseball experience.

28
00:02:15,840 --> 00:02:19,040
But what goes on behind the scenes is just as impressive.

29
00:02:19,041 --> 00:02:32,500
The average baseball park is a city unto itself, able to provide food, shelter, medicine, sanitation, and all other necessary amenities on a massive scale.

30
00:02:32,501 --> 00:02:41,200
At a typical game, the fans will consume 26,000 hot dogs and sausages, 37,000 cups of soda

31
00:02:41,201 --> 00:02:44,440
and beer, tons of popcorn, peanuts and pretzels.

32
00:02:45,120 --> 00:02:52,460
They will utilize souvenir shops, people movers, 50-plus bathrooms, where 40,000 gallons of

33
00:02:52,461 --> 00:02:53,240
water will flow.

34
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:59,880
All services provided with assembly line efficiency, without the fans giving it a second thought.

35
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:06,040
But unlike a city, the average park is a single structure.

36
00:03:06,041 --> 00:03:11,880
It has more than 10,000 feet of plumber and enough railing to stretch five miles.

37
00:03:12,640 --> 00:03:20,120
Over 2,000 60-foot pilings anchor enough steel and concrete to build a 50-story building.

38
00:03:21,920 --> 00:03:24,920
Welcome to Camden Yards in Baltimore.

39
00:03:25,680 --> 00:03:29,660
Nestled between the Baltimore docks and the historic Camden Railroad Station,

40
00:03:29,661 --> 00:03:32,200
the park opened in 1992.

41
00:03:33,220 --> 00:03:35,220
Camden is a thoroughly modern facility

42
00:03:35,221 --> 00:03:37,980
with the ability to seat 48,000.

43
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:43,160
Camden Yards is a beautiful facility.

44
00:03:43,560 --> 00:03:45,860
Even without a baseball game going on,

45
00:03:45,940 --> 00:03:47,480
you know this is a baseball park.

46
00:03:48,140 --> 00:03:50,000
This place has been built for baseball,

47
00:03:50,600 --> 00:03:53,780
and it is unlikely that anything other than baseball

48
00:03:53,781 --> 00:03:55,820
will take place in this facility.

49
00:03:55,821 --> 00:04:01,320
The secret to Camden Yard's revolutionary design was hindsight.

50
00:04:02,360 --> 00:04:07,140
Parks of the past were smaller than today's stadiums, allowing a more intimate setting.

51
00:04:07,820 --> 00:04:10,340
Games were experienced, not just watched.

52
00:04:10,940 --> 00:04:16,660
By looking to great ballparks of the past, designers found the key to Camden Yard's success.

53
00:04:16,661 --> 00:04:25,560
The Camden Yards really signaled the dawn of a new era in ballpark design and bringing

54
00:04:25,561 --> 00:04:30,220
back the unique baseball park experience.

55
00:04:30,221 --> 00:04:34,660
I think it's been a wonderful element added to the game and I think when my successes

56
00:04:34,661 --> 00:04:40,520
50 to 75 years from now look back on the period, they will look back on this era the same way

57
00:04:40,521 --> 00:04:44,060
we look back at the period 1909 to 1914.

58
00:04:44,061 --> 00:04:46,360
It's been a time that's very special.

59
00:04:46,361 --> 00:04:55,780
baseball story begins in the early 1800s teams representing small towns would challenge

60
00:04:55,781 --> 00:05:01,540
neighboring communities in competitions only slightly more organized than modern schoolyard

61
00:05:01,541 --> 00:05:07,720
games but it was not until the 1850s that insightful businessmen recognize the financial

62
00:05:07,721 --> 00:05:14,160
potential and created professional teams and the novel concept of a ballpark where the fans would

63
00:05:14,161 --> 00:05:18,783
pay to see a game. Baseball ballparks started about the 1850s.

64
00:05:18,784 --> 00:05:21,020
It was a brand new game in the

65
00:05:21,021 --> 00:05:26,328
middle of the 1800s, and soon it became a professional game in the 1860s.

66
00:05:26,329 --> 00:05:27,200
They started

67
00:05:27,201 --> 00:05:31,579
building places to keep people out who didn't pay a little bit of money.

68
00:05:31,580 --> 00:05:33,160
That very act of enclosing

69
00:05:33,161 --> 00:05:37,009
the park is really the beginning of professional sports in America.

70
00:05:37,010 --> 00:05:38,560
The first enclosed ballpark

71
00:05:38,561 --> 00:05:42,520
was Union Grounds in Brooklyn, opened in 1862.

72
00:05:43,520 --> 00:05:48,600
It had been an ice skating rink for a couple of years and had bombed.

73
00:05:49,940 --> 00:05:54,000
Union Grounds would come to typify many of the early parks.

74
00:05:54,480 --> 00:05:57,400
It was small, wooden, and horseshoe-shaped.

75
00:05:58,100 --> 00:06:01,980
Seating only 1,500 people, it was considered large for the time.

76
00:06:03,220 --> 00:06:05,440
As the game's popularity grew,

77
00:06:05,441 --> 00:06:09,180
similar parks began to spring up around the eastern seaboard.

78
00:06:10,740 --> 00:06:14,500
So there was the National Association, then the National League,

79
00:06:14,920 --> 00:06:18,500
then the American Association, then the Union Association.

80
00:06:19,280 --> 00:06:23,260
So every time you have a new league, you have, depending on how many teams,

81
00:06:23,380 --> 00:06:25,380
six to ten new parks.

82
00:06:26,160 --> 00:06:28,700
A baseball park building boom was on,

83
00:06:29,060 --> 00:06:33,180
but the new ballparks faced a problem that would continue to plague baseball

84
00:06:33,181 --> 00:06:35,880
into the modern era, real estate.

85
00:06:36,740 --> 00:06:40,060
The parks had to be close enough to the cities to attract spectators.

86
00:06:40,740 --> 00:06:46,420
Even the most ardent fan would not travel long distances via horse and buggy to watch a game.

87
00:06:47,160 --> 00:06:52,820
But finding a cheap piece of land close to the city, large enough to hold a ballpark, was difficult.

88
00:06:53,600 --> 00:06:55,100
Baseball learned to compromise.

89
00:06:55,980 --> 00:07:00,440
Ballparks such as National League Park in Philadelphia, later renamed the Baker Bowl,

90
00:07:00,441 --> 00:07:03,500
were shoehorned into already crowded downtown areas,

91
00:07:03,860 --> 00:07:07,500
giving each park its own distinctive shape and character.

92
00:07:08,180 --> 00:07:11,700
Baker Bowl started in the 1890s.

93
00:07:12,060 --> 00:07:15,100
It was a ballpark where the surrounding streets

94
00:07:15,101 --> 00:07:17,240
determined the size of the ballpark

95
00:07:17,241 --> 00:07:19,500
and determined the shape of the ballpark.

96
00:07:20,340 --> 00:07:23,300
The distance from home plate to the right field fence

97
00:07:23,301 --> 00:07:26,540
was very short, less than 300 feet.

98
00:07:26,541 --> 00:07:28,420
The distance was so short

99
00:07:28,421 --> 00:07:34,060
that a 40-foot-high right-field fence had to be constructed to limit the number of home runs.

100
00:07:35,260 --> 00:07:39,760
It might be exaggerating to say the outfield wall cast a shadow across the infield,

101
00:07:39,920 --> 00:07:43,620
but if the right fielder had eaten onions at lunch, the second baseman knew it.

102
00:07:43,980 --> 00:07:45,240
Red Smith, sports writer.

103
00:07:46,220 --> 00:07:51,980
Initially, the ballparks were utilitarian, simple structures made of wood to seat the fans.

104
00:07:52,480 --> 00:07:56,160
But that would change with Boston's south end grounds.

105
00:07:56,161 --> 00:08:04,860
The first real breakthrough in ballpark construction, when ballparks became an art, was South End Grounds in Boston.

106
00:08:05,500 --> 00:08:08,470
That was a beautiful ballpark. It had these witches' peaks.

107
00:08:08,471 --> 00:08:09,880
They're just for decoration.

108
00:08:10,580 --> 00:08:13,560
And that's the first time that owners really spent on decorating their parks.

109
00:08:14,160 --> 00:08:21,840
Like a scene from Camelot, the South End Grounds looked more like a place where armored knights, rather than baseball players, would do battle.

110
00:08:21,841 --> 00:08:29,160
initially built in 1871. By 1877, after continual renovation, the two-tiered

111
00:08:29,161 --> 00:08:33,720
grandstands and adjoining bleachers had a seating capacity of 5,000.

112
00:08:33,721 --> 00:08:41,020
When you see that park with those witches' peaks, those conical tops on it, you know

113
00:08:41,021 --> 00:08:45,900
that this is not just another wooden slap-it-up-in-a-couple-of-weeks kind of a job.

114
00:08:45,901 --> 00:08:52,900
This was clearly meant to present the ballpark as an entertainment palace.

115
00:08:52,901 --> 00:08:58,900
As the crowds continued to grow and the parks were regularly altered to seat more fans,

116
00:08:58,901 --> 00:09:02,900
some of the remedies to overcrowding would be unheard of today.

117
00:09:02,901 --> 00:09:05,900
In those early days the crowd was allowed in the field.

118
00:09:05,901 --> 00:09:08,900
There was not that much attention to pay to the home runs,

119
00:09:08,901 --> 00:09:12,900
so people were allowed to stand at the back of the park against the outfield wall,

120
00:09:12,901 --> 00:09:20,120
the outfield wall, maybe restrained by a rope, but pretty much they had free reign on games

121
00:09:20,121 --> 00:09:23,500
that were popular and sold out, but the standing room was actually on the field itself.

122
00:09:24,580 --> 00:09:28,840
The polo grounds at Coogan's Bluff, just across the Harlem River from Manhattan,

123
00:09:29,400 --> 00:09:36,160
was constructed in 1891. Originally seating 5,000 fans through many incarnations,

124
00:09:36,161 --> 00:09:41,820
the park was eventually able to seat 16,000 fans by the end of the 19th century.

125
00:09:42,540 --> 00:09:47,160
Growing popularity in larger, more opulent facilities were a boon to baseball,

126
00:09:47,600 --> 00:09:52,600
but these outward successes masked a menacing, sometimes deadly threat, fire.

127
00:09:53,220 --> 00:09:55,500
Obviously the earliest ballparks were made of wood,

128
00:09:56,360 --> 00:09:58,620
and because of that they didn't last too long.

129
00:09:58,760 --> 00:10:03,440
Virtually every early park lasted maybe eight to ten years before fire took them down.

130
00:10:04,160 --> 00:10:07,360
Most of the fires were started accidentally by the fans,

131
00:10:07,760 --> 00:10:10,740
with carelessly discarded cigars or smoldering matches.

132
00:10:10,741 --> 00:10:15,740
The 1890s saw at least 21 ballparks burst into flames.

133
00:10:15,741 --> 00:10:20,740
During one particularly bad season, 20 people lost their lives.

134
00:10:20,741 --> 00:10:26,740
If baseball were to continue to grow, the parks would need to be both larger and safer.

135
00:10:26,741 --> 00:10:34,740
It would take a leap forward in construction technology to propel baseball into its golden age.

136
00:10:34,741 --> 00:10:41,000
By the turn of the century, baseball was well established.

137
00:10:41,700 --> 00:10:44,840
Thousands flocked to parks up and down the eastern seaboard.

138
00:10:45,460 --> 00:10:49,360
The simple wooden structures couldn't contain the game's growing popularity.

139
00:10:50,500 --> 00:10:52,140
Fire was not the only hazard.

140
00:10:52,660 --> 00:10:56,720
In August of 1903, the stands in Philadelphia's Baker Bowl collapsed,

141
00:10:57,280 --> 00:10:59,600
killing 12 and injuring 100.

142
00:11:00,740 --> 00:11:03,680
That's one of the dark sides of ballparks in general.

143
00:11:03,681 --> 00:11:08,360
is fans who are either seriously injured or die,

144
00:11:09,020 --> 00:11:11,900
falling out of stands or having stands collapse.

145
00:11:12,460 --> 00:11:15,680
So it got to be a serious, serious endeavor then.

146
00:11:17,340 --> 00:11:21,020
Anything that's concerned with human life and money is generally serious.

147
00:11:22,120 --> 00:11:25,840
Baseball had outgrown its quaint old wooden ballparks.

148
00:11:26,500 --> 00:11:29,980
The next generation of parks would follow the lead of other industries

149
00:11:29,981 --> 00:11:33,700
and take advantage of relatively new construction materials.

150
00:11:34,320 --> 00:11:38,840
Pennsylvania, home to America's booming steel industry, would be at the forefront.

151
00:11:39,580 --> 00:11:44,780
Games would now be played in stadiums, built from steel and reinforced concrete.

152
00:11:45,660 --> 00:11:52,980
The thing about steel and concrete was that you could take this and round it behind home plate

153
00:11:54,620 --> 00:11:58,380
gradually to get all kinds of wonderful seats behind home plate.

154
00:11:58,381 --> 00:12:06,380
And with concrete and steel, of course, came the possibility of double-decking the park.

155
00:12:06,720 --> 00:12:09,900
And, of course, you could extend the wings up the left and right field lines.

156
00:12:10,460 --> 00:12:12,580
And that was the real genius of it.

157
00:12:13,240 --> 00:12:23,600
It is also no coincidence at all that the first two steel and concrete parks built from scratch were also built in Pennsylvania.

158
00:12:23,601 --> 00:12:31,600
Philadelphia's Scheib Park, named after team owner Ben Scheib, became the first steel and concrete stadium.

159
00:12:31,601 --> 00:12:37,600
Capable of seating 30,000 fans, this park retained its intimate surroundings,

160
00:12:37,601 --> 00:12:43,600
but also boasted such improvements as ramps, rooms for umpires and visiting teams,

161
00:12:43,601 --> 00:12:47,600
and a copula, the precursor to the modern skybox.

162
00:12:48,260 --> 00:12:50,520
Constructed in the French Renaissance style,

163
00:12:50,660 --> 00:12:55,060
the park featured decorative concrete trim over every archway and window.

164
00:12:56,620 --> 00:13:00,740
It didn't look like just an ordinary place where baseball games were played.

165
00:13:00,741 --> 00:13:04,860
It looked like places where we were almost for a religious ceremony.

166
00:13:06,160 --> 00:13:11,440
That's because around 1910, baseball was peaking in terms of its popularity.

167
00:13:12,660 --> 00:13:17,520
Its popularity began really accelerating around 1908.

168
00:13:17,600 --> 00:13:25,580
Until 1910, 19-teens, baseball was the national game without question.

169
00:13:27,620 --> 00:13:29,800
Scheib Park reflected this.

170
00:13:29,900 --> 00:13:33,360
It was a temple to this new sport that had taken over the country.

171
00:13:34,320 --> 00:13:44,736
Scheib's original right field fence stood 12 feet tall, allowing enterprising residents who lived on the adjoining street to watch home games from the comfort of their

172
00:13:44,737 --> 00:13:45,437
own roofs.

173
00:13:46,240 --> 00:13:51,140
Some even built bleachers, which began to cut into ballpark revenue.

174
00:13:51,920 --> 00:13:58,520
The solution came in 1935 in the form of a 22-foot vertical extension to the existing wall,

175
00:13:59,060 --> 00:14:03,200
a wall locals continue to refer to as the spite fence.

176
00:14:04,160 --> 00:14:11,900
Carl Scheib and his partner Connie Mack put together the first original steel and concrete park, which opened in 1909.

177
00:14:11,901 --> 00:14:19,040
And that is really the first, quote, modern ballpark, unquote.

178
00:14:19,780 --> 00:14:24,540
I should also add that it came after 1908, the year that Take Me Out to the Ball Game was written,

179
00:14:24,940 --> 00:14:30,920
also the year that the first real wonderful baseball cards were printed by American Tobacco.

180
00:14:31,480 --> 00:14:32,660
So that was a big year.

181
00:14:33,380 --> 00:14:39,400
And with baseball on the front page in eight cities, you can see that everybody and his brother was saying,

182
00:14:39,401 --> 00:14:46,680
oh boy oh boy now we're going to build a big ballpark and that really is the story from 1909

183
00:14:46,681 --> 00:14:47,443
to 1915.

184
00:14:47,444 --> 00:14:55,640
the next five years were a golden age for ballpark construction no fewer than 11 parks

185
00:14:55,641 --> 00:15:05,240
with legendary names such as wrigley ebbets and fenway would rise as shrines to america's love

186
00:15:05,241 --> 00:15:06,183
for the game.

187
00:15:06,183 --> 00:15:12,200
When you talk about the the urban ballparks like Ebbets Field, Forbes Field, Fenway

188
00:15:12,201 --> 00:15:19,340
Park, you're talking about ballparks that were right in a neighborhood and so it would stand to

189
00:15:19,341 --> 00:15:23,178
reason that there would be more of a community feeling.

190
00:15:23,179 --> 00:15:25,760
The most unique thing about all those

191
00:15:25,761 --> 00:15:33,140
parts to me is the fact that they were fit on an urban parcel, which made for some unique

192
00:15:33,141 --> 00:15:41,020
angles, and the fact that they tended to go straight up on the sides so that instead of

193
00:15:41,021 --> 00:15:46,820
going back and pushing the upper deck farther away from the playing field, you had an upper

194
00:15:46,821 --> 00:15:51,940
deck in addition to a lower deck that was very close to the field and ultimately a more

195
00:15:51,941 --> 00:15:54,140
intimate experience.

196
00:15:54,141 --> 00:15:59,100
But more than just the architecture was changing the ballpark experience.

197
00:15:59,101 --> 00:16:05,020
One man's vision would forever alter the way fans enjoy the game.

198
00:16:05,021 --> 00:16:08,600
The ballpark also had its own ambiance by the turn of the century.

199
00:16:08,601 --> 00:16:14,040
A lot of that was the result of the work of one man, Harry M. Stevens.

200
00:16:14,041 --> 00:16:19,540
He was an immigrant, came to the United States in the 1870s.

201
00:16:19,541 --> 00:16:25,540
Stevens was a lay minister, making a meager salary selling books in Columbus, Ohio.

202
00:16:25,541 --> 00:16:29,760
Seeing how well he sold books, a couple of businessmen approached him about selling their

203
00:16:29,761 --> 00:16:33,360
scorecards at a local baseball game.

204
00:16:33,361 --> 00:16:35,160
Stevens jumped at the chance.

205
00:16:35,161 --> 00:16:40,020
Shortly thereafter, he negotiated with the team owner the rights to sell and publish

206
00:16:40,021 --> 00:16:42,860
scorecards at the game.

207
00:16:42,861 --> 00:16:48,060
That became so lucrative, how he moved to New York and started doing it with the major

208
00:16:48,061 --> 00:16:49,340
ballparks.

209
00:16:49,341 --> 00:16:52,760
By the end of the century he was also selling ice cream.

210
00:16:52,761 --> 00:16:56,760
By that time he was also known as the scorecard man, Harry Stevens the scorecard man.

211
00:16:56,761 --> 00:17:01,300
But he started selling food, basically ice cream, and on one particular day it was particularly

212
00:17:01,301 --> 00:17:03,160
cold.

213
00:17:03,161 --> 00:17:07,720
So he sent one of his employees around the corner to a market to buy some, what they

214
00:17:07,721 --> 00:17:13,620
called at that time, Dachshund or Dachshund sausages and Vienna rolls.

215
00:17:13,621 --> 00:17:17,640
They cooked them up and he sent his people up into the stands yelling, get your Red Hots,

216
00:17:17,641 --> 00:17:18,940
get your Red Hots.

217
00:17:18,941 --> 00:17:23,940
So that's how the hot dog became a part of the American baseball scene.

218
00:17:23,941 --> 00:17:28,940
Stephen's menus expanded and his food service became big business.

219
00:17:28,941 --> 00:17:30,940
It continues to this day.

220
00:17:30,941 --> 00:17:37,940
But he wasn't alone in transforming a modest idea into part of baseball lore.

221
00:17:37,941 --> 00:17:43,940
The stadiums that would become American icons and would eventually embody the spirit of baseball

222
00:17:43,941 --> 00:17:46,740
were first and foremost business ventures

223
00:17:46,741 --> 00:17:49,220
with surprisingly humble beginnings.

224
00:17:49,920 --> 00:17:53,320
1913, Charles Ebbets built Ebbets Field in Brooklyn,

225
00:17:53,880 --> 00:17:57,660
and it was in a section of Brooklyn called Pig Town.

226
00:17:58,720 --> 00:18:00,680
It was an open garbage pit.

227
00:18:02,080 --> 00:18:05,520
Well, what was so great about an open garbage pit?

228
00:18:05,880 --> 00:18:07,420
Low real estate prices.

229
00:18:08,920 --> 00:18:11,860
Other landmark ballparks owe their existence

230
00:18:11,861 --> 00:18:13,660
to more than just baseball.

231
00:18:13,940 --> 00:18:20,940
Fenway Park, if you know what a fen is, it's a bog.

232
00:18:20,941 --> 00:18:25,940
And it was owned by the family that owned the Boston Globe, and it was a real estate

233
00:18:25,941 --> 00:18:28,200
scam, in essence.

234
00:18:28,201 --> 00:18:35,940
They couldn't move this land, so Junior Taylor decided to buy the Red Sox, build them a ballpark,

235
00:18:35,941 --> 00:18:39,940
sell it at a profit, and get out, which is exactly what happened.

236
00:18:39,941 --> 00:18:45,680
Though less than ideally located, the parks utilized state-of-the-art construction techniques.

237
00:18:46,560 --> 00:18:52,220
Structural steel and reinforced concrete allowed the parks to be stronger and, to the owner's delight, larger.

238
00:18:53,100 --> 00:18:58,260
They were more easily erected, durable, and had the added bonus of being fireproof,

239
00:18:58,740 --> 00:19:03,860
qualities necessary for the next step in ballpark evolution, the super stadium.

240
00:19:03,861 --> 00:19:10,860
With baseball's growing popularity, even larger stadiums were needed.

241
00:19:10,861 --> 00:19:15,860
Existing parks were regularly altered to increase their seating capacity.

242
00:19:15,861 --> 00:19:22,860
But building additional seats into the already shoehorned parks made for some interesting configurations.

243
00:19:22,861 --> 00:19:27,860
A number of the traditional, the older ballpacks had some idiosyncrasies.

244
00:19:27,861 --> 00:19:32,860
The polo grounds, the clubhouse was in center field, so after the game the players from both teams had to run out

245
00:19:32,861 --> 00:19:38,700
to run out and scoot across the field up the stairs behind the centerfield's grandstand.

246
00:19:39,660 --> 00:19:45,060
The Fenway Park left field had a hill. Also, I believe Crosley Field did as well.

247
00:19:45,061 --> 00:19:46,060
The same thing,

248
00:19:46,061 --> 00:19:50,462
they had a berm that actually ran up to the outfield fence.

249
00:19:50,463 --> 00:19:52,700
Park owners had to be careful.

250
00:19:53,340 --> 00:19:59,100
The idiosyncrasies which gave the parks their unique feel were a windfall to crafty fans.

251
00:19:59,101 --> 00:20:07,200
A lot of people watched the ballgames for free at the polar grounds from Coogan's Bluff,

252
00:20:07,760 --> 00:20:15,140
which was a high rise of a hill that went well above the top of the stadium,

253
00:20:15,440 --> 00:20:21,240
so that you were behind home plate, and if you look down, you see the roof of the ballpark.

254
00:20:21,920 --> 00:20:25,040
The revamping of old parks could only go so far.

255
00:20:25,041 --> 00:20:30,960
New construction technology allowed the first of a number of super stadiums to be built.

256
00:20:31,800 --> 00:20:38,000
Constructed in 1915, Braves Field in Boston was the world's largest ballpark to date.

257
00:20:38,660 --> 00:20:47,200
Costing over $1 million and equal in size to an 18-hole golf course, the stadium sat 42,000.

258
00:20:47,940 --> 00:20:54,480
The brainchild of Braves owner James Gaffney, the park was designed to create his favorite type of plays,

259
00:20:54,481 --> 00:20:56,940
Inside the park, home runs.

260
00:20:57,380 --> 00:21:03,380
With a distance of 402 feet down each foul line and an immense 550 feet to center field,

261
00:21:03,720 --> 00:21:09,780
not one home run cleared the fences in Braves Field during its first 10 years in use.

262
00:21:11,300 --> 00:21:14,420
That was before the home run became very popular,

263
00:21:14,700 --> 00:21:18,520
and baseball at that time was a baseball of one-run games.

264
00:21:18,900 --> 00:21:22,540
Guys getting on first, trying to steal second, hit and run,

265
00:21:22,541 --> 00:21:25,080
trying to get one run at a time,

266
00:21:25,700 --> 00:21:28,440
and Bravesfield was built on the basis

267
00:21:28,441 --> 00:21:30,300
that this is the way you played baseball.

268
00:21:30,420 --> 00:21:31,720
You didn't try to hit home runs.

269
00:21:32,040 --> 00:21:33,880
You tried to get doubles and triples

270
00:21:33,881 --> 00:21:35,700
and steal bases and so on.

271
00:21:36,720 --> 00:21:39,300
Then, when you tried to steal second

272
00:21:39,301 --> 00:21:41,200
and the ball went past the second baseman

273
00:21:41,201 --> 00:21:41,960
into the outfield,

274
00:21:42,040 --> 00:21:43,140
you might get all the way home

275
00:21:43,141 --> 00:21:45,120
before the outfielder could get to it.

276
00:21:45,560 --> 00:21:47,700
In contrast, an even larger stadium

277
00:21:47,701 --> 00:21:50,020
was being constructed that would become famous

278
00:21:50,021 --> 00:21:51,720
for the number of home runs hit

279
00:21:51,721 --> 00:21:53,820
as well as the men who hit them.

280
00:21:54,360 --> 00:22:00,620
A new era began in 1922 when the Yankees made their move from the 30,000-seat polo grounds,

281
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:06,480
which they shared with the New York Giants, to the 75,000-seat Yankee Stadium.

282
00:22:07,460 --> 00:22:12,920
What would seem to be a natural move for the Yankees was more an exercise in survival.

283
00:22:13,860 --> 00:22:19,880
In 1921, a jealous John McGraw, owner of the polo grounds and rival New York Giants,

284
00:22:19,881 --> 00:22:23,540
evicted the Yankees from their uptown Manhattan location.

285
00:22:24,500 --> 00:22:28,180
McGraw was unable to contend with the Yankees' growing gate receipts

286
00:22:28,181 --> 00:22:32,280
and popularity of their star slugger, George Herman Ruth.

287
00:22:33,180 --> 00:22:35,900
McGraw's plan for the Yankees was simple.

288
00:22:36,700 --> 00:22:41,380
If we kick them out, they won't be able to find another location on Manhattan Island.

289
00:22:41,920 --> 00:22:44,260
They'll have to move to the Bronx or Long Island.

290
00:22:44,820 --> 00:22:47,680
The fans will forget all about them and they'll be through.

291
00:22:47,681 --> 00:22:53,140
The Yankees fought back by constructing the greatest baseball stadium ever seen.

292
00:22:53,680 --> 00:22:59,820
The location chosen just across the Harlem River, within eyesight of the polo grounds.

293
00:23:00,540 --> 00:23:06,380
Yankee Stadium was a pinnacle and maybe the pinnacle for ballpark construction.

294
00:23:07,280 --> 00:23:09,460
It was the first triple-decker stadium.

295
00:23:09,461 --> 00:23:12,600
It was the first huge stadium.

296
00:23:12,601 --> 00:23:21,840
65,000 and change, 70,000, 78,000 for some games, even though the fire marshals were

297
00:23:21,841 --> 00:23:22,541
petrified.

298
00:23:23,520 --> 00:23:26,460
And it was all made possible by Babe Ruth.

299
00:23:26,461 --> 00:23:29,460
So it's called the house that Ruth built.

300
00:23:29,461 --> 00:23:42,700
Ah, Homer, the first in Yankee Stadium, the first of many for Babe Ruth.

301
00:23:43,840 --> 00:23:45,860
1923 is a great year for Ruth.

302
00:23:46,340 --> 00:23:50,280
He sets his all-time batting mark, and the Yanks win their first World Series.

303
00:23:50,560 --> 00:23:56,200
Though credited to the Sultan of Swat, the true builders of the stadium were the team

304
00:23:56,201 --> 00:24:01,200
of New York-based White Construction Company and Cleveland's Osborne Engineering.

305
00:24:01,201 --> 00:24:07,200
Osborne's prior baseball park experience had made it the logical choice for the job.

306
00:24:07,201 --> 00:24:15,700
Osborne Engineering Company, from the turn of the century, starting with the

307
00:24:15,701 --> 00:24:23,200
Polar Grounds in New York, designed the Forrest Field, Ebbets Field, Fenway Park, Yankee Stadium.

308
00:24:23,201 --> 00:24:26,480
did over 100 stadiums over the years.

309
00:24:27,500 --> 00:24:28,560
Osborne did the plans,

310
00:24:28,700 --> 00:24:30,240
White Construction did the construction,

311
00:24:31,240 --> 00:24:32,500
promised to have it done in a year,

312
00:24:32,620 --> 00:24:33,640
did it in 11 months,

313
00:24:34,280 --> 00:24:35,020
under budget,

314
00:24:35,640 --> 00:24:39,240
just about the smoothest job going.

315
00:24:40,120 --> 00:24:41,460
And the numbers are staggering,

316
00:24:41,620 --> 00:24:42,420
the amount of wood,

317
00:24:42,520 --> 00:24:43,340
the amount of concrete,

318
00:24:43,500 --> 00:24:44,260
the amount of steel.

319
00:24:44,820 --> 00:24:47,580
2,200 tons of structural steel

320
00:24:47,581 --> 00:24:49,860
and 28,000 cubic yards

321
00:24:49,861 --> 00:24:51,240
of reinforced concrete

322
00:24:51,241 --> 00:24:57,067
were needed to construct the stadium which occupied 10 acres of land.

323
00:24:57,068 --> 00:24:58,840
An additional 950,000

324
00:24:58,841 --> 00:25:02,831
board feet of lumber were used to construct the bleachers.

325
00:25:02,832 --> 00:25:05,240
The two million dollar facility was

326
00:25:05,241 --> 00:25:12,520
the first venue to be called a stadium where fans watched men with names like DiMaggio,

327
00:25:12,521 --> 00:25:17,520
Gary and Ruth become baseball legends.

328
00:25:17,521 --> 00:25:20,520
In baseball, the only thing bigger than Yankee Stadium

329
00:25:20,521 --> 00:25:22,520
were the men who played there.

330
00:25:22,521 --> 00:25:25,520
Yankee Stadium is significant in history

331
00:25:25,521 --> 00:25:28,520
because Babe Ruth played in Yankee Stadium.

332
00:25:28,521 --> 00:25:31,520
And Babe Ruth was the greatest baseball player by far

333
00:25:31,521 --> 00:25:33,520
that has ever lived.

334
00:25:33,521 --> 00:25:37,520
After that, Joe DiMaggio took over in the outfield.

335
00:25:37,521 --> 00:25:41,720
And after that, Mickey Mouse.

336
00:25:43,040 --> 00:25:47,700
So greatness is on the turf at Yankee Stadium.

337
00:25:47,880 --> 00:25:49,440
That's why it's so famous.

338
00:25:50,100 --> 00:25:54,500
The stadiums and super stadiums constructed in the first quarter of the century

339
00:25:54,501 --> 00:25:56,880
would serve baseball for decades.

340
00:25:57,580 --> 00:26:02,600
From 1923 to 1952, only one baseball stadium was built,

341
00:26:03,340 --> 00:26:05,060
Cleveland's Municipal Stadium.

342
00:26:05,061 --> 00:26:08,460
but baseball itself did not stagnate.

343
00:26:08,461 --> 00:26:15,760
In 1935, one technical achievement would forever change the way we watch the game.

344
00:26:15,761 --> 00:26:17,260
Lights.

345
00:26:17,261 --> 00:26:23,360
The first night game was played at Crossley Field in Cincinnati, May 24, 1935.

346
00:26:23,361 --> 00:26:28,060
And President Roosevelt threw the switch in the White House, activating the lights.

347
00:26:28,061 --> 00:26:35,760
Obviously, there was tremendous value to having people come to the ballpark at the end of the workday.

348
00:26:36,720 --> 00:26:41,740
And it wasn't friendly for kids, but it was great for adults, working people.

349
00:26:42,740 --> 00:26:48,780
With the light came larger crowds, and the old stadiums needed to make way for the new.

350
00:26:49,400 --> 00:26:53,720
An era ended in 1960 with the raising of Ebbets Field in Brooklyn.

351
00:26:53,721 --> 00:26:58,700
And now it's play ball again, but not the sort Dodger fans cheer.

352
00:27:04,040 --> 00:27:07,700
This time, Ebbets Field itself is struck out.

353
00:27:09,380 --> 00:27:14,500
As the venues aged, the cost of operations and upkeep increased.

354
00:27:15,180 --> 00:27:18,160
Due to economic difficulties by the 1960s,

355
00:27:18,161 --> 00:27:24,800
team owners and city officials thought it best to create one home for all professional sporting activities,

356
00:27:25,500 --> 00:27:27,500
thus spreading the financial burden.

357
00:27:30,020 --> 00:27:35,540
By the 1960s, parks built in the first quarter of the century were deteriorating.

358
00:27:36,240 --> 00:27:39,320
The stadiums were becoming more and more expensive to maintain.

359
00:27:39,960 --> 00:27:42,280
Always on the lookout for a way to save money,

360
00:27:42,281 --> 00:27:49,240
team owners joined local politicians and decided the new stadiums needed to be multi-purpose facilities.

361
00:27:50,740 --> 00:27:54,400
You have ball clubs that are saying,

362
00:27:54,540 --> 00:27:58,520
now we're in ballparks that are pushing 60 years old.

363
00:27:59,040 --> 00:28:00,640
They're starting to show their age.

364
00:28:01,920 --> 00:28:05,960
Our people want a nicer ambiance to go to the ballpark.

365
00:28:06,440 --> 00:28:10,280
To combat ever-rising real estate prices for the first time,

366
00:28:10,281 --> 00:28:15,680
owners looked outside of city limits for places to build their new modern stadiums.

367
00:28:15,681 --> 00:28:20,320
The stadiums that started to be built in the 50s and the 60s and the 70s

368
00:28:20,321 --> 00:28:28,280
tended to be located outside of cities on large plots of land that planners call super blocks,

369
00:28:28,840 --> 00:28:31,860
which meant that the stadium was not constrained by anything.

370
00:28:32,240 --> 00:28:34,980
You had the stadium located on the super blocks surrounded by parking,

371
00:28:35,820 --> 00:28:39,200
and typically sites were chosen for their proximity to freeways.

372
00:28:39,201 --> 00:28:45,200
The new parks sacrifice the ambiance and intimacy of the older parks.

373
00:28:45,201 --> 00:28:52,260
There is a space age quality to them, you know, spaceship looking kind of places, lots

374
00:28:52,261 --> 00:28:56,320
of concrete, lots of plastic in the seating bowl.

375
00:28:56,321 --> 00:29:01,860
A real departure from the older ballparks.

376
00:29:01,861 --> 00:29:07,320
And so they're reflective, I think, of the time period that they were built.

377
00:29:07,321 --> 00:29:11,740
for a certain time period served a very definite purpose.

378
00:29:12,320 --> 00:29:16,900
One of the most otherworldly looking stadiums is Houston's Astrodome,

379
00:29:17,220 --> 00:29:19,897
dubbed the eighth wonder of the world.

380
00:29:19,898 --> 00:29:23,700
Construction on the 74,000 seat stadium began in 1962.

381
00:29:24,400 --> 00:29:29,880
At a cost of $35 million, the Astrodome ushered in a new era of baseball parks.

382
00:29:30,320 --> 00:29:35,580
Its builders created an 18-story high dome ceiling that could house baseball, football,

383
00:29:35,581 --> 00:29:40,500
basketball and countless other events. Like many of the newer stadiums, the

384
00:29:40,501 --> 00:29:45,540
Astrodome seemed to suffer from an identity crisis. The new breed of

385
00:29:45,541 --> 00:29:49,680
stadiums were built to save money but they were not without their luxuries.

386
00:29:49,681 --> 00:29:56,880
The Astrodome introduced the very first skybox. The exclusive skyboxes, a place

387
00:29:56,881 --> 00:30:02,700
for relaxation and entertainment. Individually decorated suites with

388
00:30:02,701 --> 00:30:09,200
built-in closed circuit TV. This is really some kind of baseball.

389
00:30:09,201 --> 00:30:12,000
The success of the Astrodome

390
00:30:12,001 --> 00:30:17,022
Skybox created the desire for skyboxes in most of the parks to follow.

391
00:30:17,023 --> 00:30:18,600
The skyboxes represent

392
00:30:18,601 --> 00:30:21,166
the ultimate in creature comforts.

393
00:30:21,167 --> 00:30:25,620
They became a huge success, but not all innovations were so

394
00:30:25,621 --> 00:30:26,552
well received.

395
00:30:26,553 --> 00:30:31,880
When the old ballparks started to crumble, they were replaced with multi-purpose

396
00:30:31,881 --> 00:30:34,980
stadiums in which both baseball and football were played.

397
00:30:36,260 --> 00:30:42,780
Unfortunately for baseball, the spectator demands between the two sports were very different.

398
00:30:44,480 --> 00:30:50,200
Which is easy enough to visualize once you think about the playing fields of the two sports.

399
00:30:51,580 --> 00:30:57,100
Huge majority of the seats are nowhere near the infield and therefore it took away the intimacy

400
00:30:57,101 --> 00:30:57,860
of the game.

401
00:30:57,861 --> 00:31:02,120
Another complaint about the Astrodome involved the field itself.

402
00:31:02,700 --> 00:31:05,920
Its evolution was due to an unforeseen chain of events.

403
00:31:06,420 --> 00:31:10,500
Originally, the dome's translucent ceiling panels created so much glare

404
00:31:10,501 --> 00:31:13,420
that players were blinded when they looked up.

405
00:31:14,280 --> 00:31:18,500
To reduce the glare, a translucent acrylic coating was applied to the panels.

406
00:31:19,740 --> 00:31:23,680
The glare was reduced, but the lack of light killed the grass.

407
00:31:24,540 --> 00:31:28,100
The sod was removed and a plastic grass carpet was installed.

408
00:31:29,120 --> 00:31:31,780
The synthetic surface was dubbed astroturf,

409
00:31:32,260 --> 00:31:36,360
and many players claimed it was like playing baseball on concrete.

410
00:31:37,400 --> 00:31:42,240
For purists, this mix of baseball and technology was near sacrilege,

411
00:31:42,241 --> 00:31:45,920
but there was no refuting that this was the wave of the future.

412
00:31:46,880 --> 00:31:50,820
The Astrodome featured the world's first exploding scoreboard.

413
00:31:50,821 --> 00:31:59,820
More than half an acre of computer sequence lights, 14,000 lights in all, would raise the ballpark experience to a new level.

414
00:31:59,821 --> 00:32:02,820
It's a home run!

415
00:32:20,820 --> 00:32:34,820
The late 1960s brought about cookie-cutter, multi-purpose facilities with names like Riverfront Stadium,

416
00:32:38,280 --> 00:32:40,540
Three Rivers Stadium, and Veterans Stadium.

417
00:32:40,541 --> 00:32:47,320
They each seated 50,000 fans and had symmetrical playing surfaces covered with astroturf.

418
00:32:48,320 --> 00:32:52,040
The new stadiums also shared another unwanted trait.

419
00:32:52,440 --> 00:32:55,740
Instead of fans identifying a park with its individual owner,

420
00:32:56,020 --> 00:33:00,400
they now had to identify with a faceless, bottom-line-oriented corporation.

421
00:33:01,180 --> 00:33:03,900
One of the things about these concrete donuts, as I call them,

422
00:33:04,600 --> 00:33:08,720
was the fact that they were no longer built by one person.

423
00:33:08,721 --> 00:33:15,960
It wasn't Scheib Park, Comiskey Park, Wrigley Field, Ebbets Field, Griffith Stadium.

424
00:33:15,961 --> 00:33:23,120
It was some absolutely atrocious, safe-as-hell corporate name.

425
00:33:23,940 --> 00:33:25,280
Riverfront Stadium.

426
00:33:25,900 --> 00:33:28,240
Sounds like a goddamn housing development.

427
00:33:28,940 --> 00:33:30,400
Three Rivers Stadium.

428
00:33:30,840 --> 00:33:31,940
Veterans Stadium.

429
00:33:32,820 --> 00:33:36,320
So they were compromises right from the get-go.

430
00:33:37,300 --> 00:33:39,740
As widespread as the concrete donuts became,

431
00:33:39,860 --> 00:33:43,380
they were never accepted by the fans, at least the baseball fans,

432
00:33:43,381 --> 00:33:45,680
and were always looked upon with derision.

433
00:33:45,960 --> 00:33:47,980
I spent nine years living in Philadelphia.

434
00:33:48,740 --> 00:33:50,820
When I arrived in Philadelphia in the early 70s,

435
00:33:50,880 --> 00:33:54,560
the skeleton of Scheib Park was still there,

436
00:33:55,160 --> 00:33:59,320
obviously desolate and ready to be torn down.

437
00:33:59,780 --> 00:34:02,420
And the vet at that time was about three years old.

438
00:34:02,720 --> 00:34:05,220
I never had a problem watching a game at the vet

439
00:34:05,221 --> 00:34:06,420
because I really loved baseball,

440
00:34:06,600 --> 00:34:08,700
so as long as I was watching a game, I didn't have a problem.

441
00:34:09,400 --> 00:34:12,240
The fans weren't the only ones unhappy

442
00:34:12,241 --> 00:34:14,720
with baseball's concrete donuts.

443
00:34:15,460 --> 00:34:17,420
Richie Hebner of the Pirates always said that,

444
00:34:17,480 --> 00:34:20,460
that there were some days he'd be out in the field and forget where he was

445
00:34:20,461 --> 00:34:23,940
because he'd look around the park and he wouldn't be able to tell which stadium he was in.

446
00:34:23,941 --> 00:34:27,940
The casualties of these new modern multipurpose facilities

447
00:34:27,941 --> 00:34:31,440
were the quaint old downtown historic ballparks.

448
00:34:32,260 --> 00:34:36,980
One by one, teams left parks from the golden era of ballpark construction,

449
00:34:37,520 --> 00:34:40,200
which were not only replaced, but demolished.

450
00:34:41,560 --> 00:34:43,940
Cincinnati's Crossley Field, built in 1912,

451
00:34:44,720 --> 00:34:46,920
was raised in 1970.

452
00:34:50,360 --> 00:34:51,980
Pittsburgh's Forbes Field,

453
00:34:52,400 --> 00:34:54,980
the second stadium built out of steel and concrete,

454
00:34:55,400 --> 00:34:57,180
was demolished in 1971.

455
00:34:58,200 --> 00:35:00,140
And Philadelphia's Scheib Park,

456
00:35:00,440 --> 00:35:02,600
the very first concrete and steel park,

457
00:35:02,700 --> 00:35:04,580
was demolished in 1976.

458
00:35:06,020 --> 00:35:09,220
The 1960s were the dark ages of ballpark construction.

459
00:35:10,260 --> 00:35:12,540
Our nation was tired of chaos,

460
00:35:12,541 --> 00:35:14,400
and the old ballparks were kind of chaotic.

461
00:35:14,401 --> 00:35:15,980
They had irregular fences.

462
00:35:16,520 --> 00:35:17,880
You never knew what to expect.

463
00:35:18,080 --> 00:35:20,520
There were funny bounces on the outfield walls.

464
00:35:20,600 --> 00:35:22,000
There were funny bounces on the grass.

465
00:35:22,500 --> 00:35:26,300
And the whole nation just decided that uniformity was beautiful,

466
00:35:26,301 --> 00:35:28,520
and you can understand by what was going on in our society.

467
00:35:29,200 --> 00:35:31,520
However, it was the dark ages of ballpark constructions

468
00:35:31,521 --> 00:35:32,620
because they were all the same.

469
00:35:33,700 --> 00:35:35,480
The building trend continued.

470
00:35:36,080 --> 00:35:41,620
From 1962 to 1989, just one baseball-only stadium was built,

471
00:35:42,220 --> 00:35:44,300
Royal Stadium in Kansas City.

472
00:35:44,400 --> 00:35:49,400
But advancements in stadium technology were still being made.

473
00:35:49,401 --> 00:35:54,400
On June 5th, 1989, the Toronto Sky Dome opened.

474
00:35:54,401 --> 00:36:02,400
Taking a page from the Astrodome, the new stadium sported a 282-foot-high dome ceiling with one improvement.

475
00:36:07,400 --> 00:36:10,400
It was convertible.

476
00:36:10,401 --> 00:36:18,460
Moving at 71 feet per minute, it takes 20 minutes for the three-piece, 11,000-ton structure to retract.

477
00:36:19,300 --> 00:36:22,520
As the two top pieces telescope backwards,

478
00:36:23,520 --> 00:36:33,180
the end of the dome rotates 180 degrees, blowing the lid off the 60,000-seat stadium.

479
00:36:33,840 --> 00:36:37,760
Though improvements like retractable roofs brought some light back to the game,

480
00:36:37,761 --> 00:36:40,560
the larger venue still lacked intimacy.

481
00:36:41,660 --> 00:36:45,620
A growing cry from fans would soon convince owners that a change was needed.

482
00:36:46,400 --> 00:36:47,620
Fans wanted something new.

483
00:36:48,220 --> 00:36:51,260
What they would get was a piece of history.

484
00:36:52,640 --> 00:36:57,900
The concrete donut stadiums had dominated baseball for nearly 30 years.

485
00:36:58,640 --> 00:37:02,140
Many complained that baseball had lost its sense of intimacy.

486
00:37:02,141 --> 00:37:09,140
In 1992, the Baltimore Orioles took a bold step forward with their creation of Camden Yards.

487
00:37:09,141 --> 00:37:15,140
It was pretty clear from the outset that the Orioles and the Maryland Stadium Authority

488
00:37:15,141 --> 00:37:19,140
wanted to do something that would set a new standard.

489
00:37:19,141 --> 00:37:26,140
Joe Speer is the chief architect for the Kansas City-based HOK Sports Facilities Group,

490
00:37:26,141 --> 00:37:30,140
the leading sports architectural firm in America.

491
00:37:30,141 --> 00:37:37,140
HOK was brought in to not only design Camden Yards, but also advise on its location.

492
00:37:37,141 --> 00:37:43,140
We actually looked, in terms of deciding the site for Camden Yards in Baltimore,

493
00:37:43,141 --> 00:37:46,664
we looked at a couple of dozen sites.

494
00:37:46,665 --> 00:37:51,140
The mayor at that point did a very smart thing.

495
00:37:51,141 --> 00:37:56,140
He asked the public for input as to where the baseball park should be built.

496
00:37:56,141 --> 00:37:56,841
be built.

497
00:37:56,842 --> 00:38:03,180
And I think we got about 27 or 28 different suggestions about where the park should

498
00:38:03,181 --> 00:38:03,881
be.

499
00:38:03,882 --> 00:38:08,300
And we thought, as did the Orioles and the Stadium Authority, that if the ballpark were

500
00:38:08,301 --> 00:38:11,320
downtown, people would make a day of it.

501
00:38:11,321 --> 00:38:15,020
Built in Baltimore's historic warehouse district,

502
00:38:15,021 --> 00:38:20,860
close to the Baltimore shoreline and parallel to the trolley car tracks used by fans at the turn

503
00:38:20,861 --> 00:38:27,340
of the century, Camden Yards is the crown jewel of baseball's attempt to create and build stadiums

504
00:38:27,341 --> 00:38:29,816
reminiscent of a bygone era.

505
00:38:29,817 --> 00:38:35,740
When you talk about the evolution of baseball parks, you start with

506
00:38:35,741 --> 00:38:42,220
your wooden facilities and then you move ahead to your steel and concrete such as Forbes Field,

507
00:38:42,221 --> 00:38:49,820
Ebbets Field, Fenway Park, so on. You then move towards the super stadium

508
00:38:49,821 --> 00:38:55,600
concepts such as a Yankee Stadium or a Cleveland Municipal Stadium. As you move

509
00:38:55,601 --> 00:39:04,120
into the 1960s you get into the cookie cutter, multi-purpose ballpark. Still

510
00:39:04,121 --> 00:39:12,100
lots of seats, less obstructed views, and obviously the ability to accommodate more

511
00:39:12,101 --> 00:39:13,600
than one sport.

512
00:39:13,601 --> 00:39:20,800
And then finally you get into kind of the retro parks, the retro classic

513
00:39:20,801 --> 00:39:23,100
parks such as Camden Yards.

514
00:39:23,101 --> 00:39:28,099
And that's really the genius of Camden Yards.

515
00:39:28,100 --> 00:39:33,320
For once, they didn't build at the intersection

516
00:39:33,321 --> 00:39:37,118
of two interstates. It was built in the city. It was part of the city.

517
00:39:37,119 --> 00:39:38,040
The city was part

518
00:39:38,041 --> 00:39:40,760
of the ballpark. The ballpark was part of the city.

519
00:39:40,761 --> 00:39:46,100
No fewer than 10 major league parks such as Cleveland's Jacobs Field and Denver's Coors

520
00:39:46,101 --> 00:39:52,280
Field have followed Camden Yard's lead with great success.

521
00:39:52,281 --> 00:39:57,760
When Denver was awarded an expansion franchise, part of the selling point was the new stadium's

522
00:39:57,761 --> 00:39:58,760
location.

523
00:39:58,761 --> 00:40:05,440
Again, HOK wanted the park downtown and reflective of the community.

524
00:40:05,441 --> 00:40:09,820
Really our challenge was to design a park that fit that community.

525
00:40:09,821 --> 00:40:21,834
The site is adjacent to the lower downtown district, and we thought the real chance for doing something incredibly meaningful for the city was really what it would do

526
00:40:21,835 --> 00:40:28,420
to the neighborhoods around the ballpark and what it would mean to the lower downtown area.

527
00:40:29,040 --> 00:40:34,020
So that really, I think, is the success story of Coors Field.

528
00:40:34,021 --> 00:40:41,020
Years of baseball experience have culminated in today's high-tech baseball park designs.

529
00:40:41,021 --> 00:40:49,020
Enron Field in Texas and Pacific Bell Park in San Francisco are baseball's newest breed of retro parks.

530
00:40:49,021 --> 00:40:54,020
The site that we're building on out here is about 12.8 acres.

531
00:40:54,021 --> 00:41:02,020
And to give you some measure of how small that is, the footprint, the physical footprint of Coors Field wouldn't fit on this site.

532
00:41:02,021 --> 00:41:02,825
this site.

533
00:41:02,826 --> 00:41:09,020
So we told the Giants that the China Basin site would be worth that challenge

534
00:41:09,520 --> 00:41:15,460
and worth that effort because of the way it makes the bay part of the game of baseball

535
00:41:15,461 --> 00:41:17,700
in San Francisco.

536
00:41:17,701 --> 00:41:24,700
Getting 40,000 people to the games on time was another concern for the Pac Bell designers.

537
00:41:24,701 --> 00:41:30,180
One of the great things about Pac Bell Park is it's going to have its own ferry dock,

538
00:41:30,181 --> 00:41:33,860
you can actually ride the ferry to the ball game.

539
00:41:33,861 --> 00:41:36,100
And I think that adds to the experience.

540
00:41:36,101 --> 00:41:41,420
I mean, people talk about, oh, I remember the subway series, or I remember riding the

541
00:41:41,421 --> 00:41:43,520
L and going to a Cubs game.

542
00:41:43,521 --> 00:41:49,740
And so not only does it help the practical, functional characteristics of getting people

543
00:41:49,741 --> 00:41:54,860
out to the park, but it adds a lot of richness and texture to their game day experience.

544
00:41:54,861 --> 00:41:57,900
Packabell Park being on the bay is going to be fantastic.

545
00:41:57,901 --> 00:41:59,900
It's going to be very special.

546
00:41:59,901 --> 00:42:01,900
In terms of our visualization process,

547
00:42:01,901 --> 00:42:05,900
we like to design projects that fit a community.

548
00:42:05,901 --> 00:42:11,900
In San Francisco, for us, that was all about San Francisco Bay.

549
00:42:11,901 --> 00:42:15,900
In Pacific Bell Stadium, the right field fence will be very short,

550
00:42:15,901 --> 00:42:17,900
and if somebody hits a home or clearing the fence,

551
00:42:17,901 --> 00:42:21,900
it may land on the bay, which will make it very interesting.

552
00:42:21,901 --> 00:42:24,900
Where else can you have home runs splashing on the ocean?

553
00:42:24,901 --> 00:42:29,900
Pacific Bell will set the pace probably for the next 10 to 20 years.

554
00:42:29,901 --> 00:42:34,900
Sixteen new multi-million dollar parks are on the drawing boards,

555
00:42:34,901 --> 00:42:39,900
and these new parks are finding their way back into the hearts of the cities.

556
00:42:39,901 --> 00:42:43,900
The goal for all modern baseball parks is to provide an intimate,

557
00:42:43,901 --> 00:42:49,900
baseball-only, fan-friendly setting reminiscent of baseball stadium's glory years

558
00:42:49,901 --> 00:42:51,900
with all of today's amenities.

559
00:42:51,901 --> 00:42:57,080
We feel passionate about ballparks because ballparks are part of our culture.

560
00:42:58,220 --> 00:43:01,200
It used to be that there was real life and then there was sports.

561
00:43:02,520 --> 00:43:04,420
Now it's a little bit of both.

562
00:43:05,640 --> 00:43:07,980
This is another sad thing about life and sports.

563
00:43:08,720 --> 00:43:15,880
But your Uncle Sid and your Aunt Thelma that you love and you see them two or three times a year,

564
00:43:16,960 --> 00:43:19,020
well, you see John Franco 80 times a year.

565
00:43:19,021 --> 00:43:23,940
or Bernie Williams or Mark McGuire.

566
00:43:24,660 --> 00:43:27,520
You know more about them than you know about your family.

567
00:43:28,520 --> 00:43:30,260
In a sense, they are your family.

568
00:43:31,400 --> 00:43:35,200
So where they, quote, live, unquote, is the ballpark.

569
00:43:36,020 --> 00:43:38,580
From the sandlots of the 19th century

570
00:43:38,581 --> 00:43:41,340
to modern technological wonders,

571
00:43:41,960 --> 00:43:44,220
baseball parks are part of our heritage.

572
00:43:45,060 --> 00:43:47,680
With each revered park that comes down,

573
00:43:47,681 --> 00:43:49,840
we lose a little innocence.

574
00:43:50,620 --> 00:43:53,660
But with each new park, we embrace the future.

575
00:43:54,520 --> 00:43:56,440
But no matter how the parks change,

576
00:43:56,820 --> 00:44:00,480
they will always be the homes to America's pastime.

577
00:44:03,780 --> 00:44:07,560
Like magic, the cloud makes the clouds roll by.

578
00:44:08,520 --> 00:44:10,260
The Bambino is back.

579
00:44:10,900 --> 00:44:12,560
The season rushes on.

580
00:45:17,680 --> 00:45:19,680
So

